   "content": ""
  },
  {
   "slug": "about",
   "title": "About",
   "featured_media": "ridge",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"large\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-large-font-size\">I am a landscape photographer working mostly within a day&rsquo;s walk of home. I came to photography late, after years of walking the same hills with a notebook, and it changed how I look at everything.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>My approach is slow. I visit a place, often without a camera, and come back when the season, the weather and the time of day line up with the picture I have in mind. Sometimes that takes a week. Sometimes it takes three years.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Exhibitions and publications</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><em>Edges</em>, a group show of coastal photography, Harbour Gallery, 2025</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><em>Twelve Months on the Ridge</em>, a small book of photographs, 2024</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Regular contributor to regional walking and nature magazines</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->"
  },
  {
   "slug": "prints",
   "title": "Prints & Commissions",
   "featured_media": "buttercups",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"large\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-large-font-size\">All photographs on this site are available as signed, limited-edition prints.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Small</strong>: 30 &times; 40 cm, edition of 50</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Medium</strong>: 50 &times; 70 cm, edition of 25</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li><strong>Large</strong>: 70 &times; 100 cm, edition of 10</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Prints are made on 100% cotton rag paper with archival pigment inks and are shipped flat, unframed, with a certificate of authenticity.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Commissions</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>I take on a small number of commissions each year, mostly for books, landscape architects and conservation charities. Tell me about the place and what you need the pictures to do.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:buttons -->\n<div class=\"wp-block-buttons\"><!-- wp:button -->\n<div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"{{page:contact}}\">Get in touch</a></div>\n<!-- /wp:button --></div>\n<!-- /wp:buttons -->"
  },
  {
   "slug": "contact",
   "title": "Contact",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph {\"fontSize\":\"large\"} -->\n<p class=\"has-large-font-size\">For prints, commissions, licensing and talks, write to <a href=\"mailto:studio@example.com\">studio@example.com</a>. I reply within a few days, unless I am somewhere without a signal, which is more often than you might think.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->"
  }
 ],
 "posts": [
  {
   "slug": "waiting-for-the-wind-to-stop",
   "title": "Waiting for the wind to stop",
   "date": "2026-05-30T20:00:00",
   "categories": [
    "stories"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "golden-hour",
    "seasons"
   ],
   "featured_media": "meadow",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>The meadow photograph on the front page took four evenings. The light was right on all four. The wind was not.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Flowers move. At a shutter speed slow enough for a small aperture and low ISO, every stem becomes a soft streak. Some photographers love that; I wanted every petal still.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>On the fourth evening the breeze dropped about ten minutes before sunset, as it often does inland. I had perhaps ninety seconds of stillness. I made eleven frames. One of them is the picture.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Landscape photography is mostly waiting, with a little bit of pressing a button at the end.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph --></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->",
   "comments": [
    {
     "author": "Jonas",
     "content": "Ninety seconds after four evenings. That is the most honest description of landscape work I have read.",
     "date": "2026-06-01T08:30:00"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "slug": "the-ridge-three-seasons",
   "title": "The ridge, three seasons",
   "date": "2026-03-08T17:00:00",
   "categories": [
    "landscapes",
    "stories"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "seasons"
   ],
   "featured_media": "ridge",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A single tree on a ridge, photographed from the same spot in winter, spring and late summer.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":{{media_id:ridge}},\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:ridge}}\" alt=\"A tree on a grassy ridge\" class=\"wp-image-{{media_id:ridge}}\"/></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Revisiting a subject is the best exercise I know. The first visit is about the place; every visit after is about the light, the season and what you have learned since.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->"
  },
  {
   "slug": "shooting-into-the-light-at-the-cliff-dwellings",
   "title": "Shooting into the light at the cliff dwellings",
   "date": "2026-04-12T07:30:00",
   "categories": [
    "technique",
    "landscapes"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "golden-hour"
   ],
   "featured_media": "cliff",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Midday at the cliff dwellings is all contrast: blinding sandstone, black shadow. At opening time, the alcove is in shade and lit by light reflected off the canyon, which is soft, warm and forgiving.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading -->\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What helped</h2>\n<!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list -->\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Arriving before the sun reached the alcove.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>Bracketing three exposures for the brightest sky.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item -->\n<li>A lens hood, and a hand held up to shade the front element.</li>\n<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>\n<!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":{{media_id:ruins}},\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} -->\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:ruins}}\" alt=\"Stone towers of the cliff village\" class=\"wp-image-{{media_id:ruins}}\"/></figure>\n<!-- /wp:image -->"
  },
  {
   "slug": "why-i-still-carry-a-polarizer",
   "title": "Why I still carry a polarizer",
   "date": "2026-02-14T12:00:00",
   "categories": [
    "technique"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "filters"
   ],
   "featured_media": "coral",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Most effects can be added later on a computer. A polarizing filter is one of the few that cannot: it removes reflections at the moment of capture, and no software can recover what the reflection hid.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>On water it lets you see the stones in a creek bed. On wet leaves after rain it brings back their color. On a blue sky it deepens the blue, though a wide lens will show it unevenly, so go gently.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote -->\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Turn the ring slowly and watch. When the glare disappears, stop.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph --></blockquote>\n<!-- /wp:quote -->"
  },
  {
   "slug": "marsh-birds-and-the-long-lens",
   "title": "Marsh birds and the long lens",
   "date": "2026-09-20T06:30:00",
   "categories": [
    "technique",
    "stories"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "long-lens"
   ],
   "featured_media": "birds",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>I am not a wildlife photographer, but a marsh at dawn is a landscape full of birds, and sometimes you want them in the frame.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>A long lens compresses distance and lets you stay well back, which matters more than any technique. If a bird changes what it is doing because of you, you are too close.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Sit still, let the birds forget you, and photograph the scene rather than the bird: reeds, water, light, and somewhere in it, a small shape feeding.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->"
  },
  {
   "slug": "a-creek-in-black-and-white",
   "title": "A creek in black and white",
   "date": "2026-10-04T15:00:00",
   "categories": [
    "landscapes"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "black-and-white"
   ],
   "featured_media": "creek",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Some days the color is the subject. Other days it gets in the way. On a grey afternoon the creek was all texture: wet stone, moving water, dark grass.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>Converting to black and white is not just removing color; it is deciding how bright each color becomes. Darkening the greens made the water glow, and the picture finally said what I saw.</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->"
  }
 ],
